<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Poetic Edda, Prose Edda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edda is the name of two medieval Icelandic works that preserve Old Norse myth and heroic legend. The Prose Edda, written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a> around 1220, is a handbook of poetics and mythology, while the Poetic Edda is a collection of older anonymous poems known mainly from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of about 1270. Together they are the main sources for Norse mythology.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edda.jpg" alt="Edda" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: W. G. Collingwood (1854-1932) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Tree_of_Yggdrasil.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Edda is an Old Norse term that modern scholars apply to two medieval Icelandic works, the Prose Edda and an older collection of poems, the Poetic Edda, which had no original title. Historically the word referred only to the Prose Edda, but that usage has fallen out of favour because of confusion with the other work. Both were recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a> in the 13th century, though they contain material from earlier traditional sources reaching back into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>. They are the main sources for the skaldic tradition of medieval Iceland and for Norse mythology.</p><p>At least five explanations of the word have been proposed. One links it to a word meaning great-grandmother in the poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigs-ula">Rígsþula</a>, another to Old Norse óðr, poetry, and a third, put forward in 1895 by Eiríkr Magnússon, to the place name Oddi, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a> was educated. The most widely accepted derives it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> edo, I compose, though this may reflect modern usage more than history. A fifth treats it as a bird title, little eider duck, like the legal code Grágás.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Prose Edda is an Icelandic manual of poetics that also contains many mythological stories. Its purpose was to enable Icelandic poets and readers to understand the subtleties of alliterative verse and the mythological allusions behind the many kennings of skaldic poetry. The Icelandic scholar and historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a> wrote it around 1220. It survives in four known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and three fragments, written from about 1300 to about 1600. It consists of a Prologue and three books: Gylfaginning, on the creation and foretold destruction and rebirth of the Norse world, Skáldskaparmál, a dialogue between the sea god Ægir and the god of poetry Bragi, and Háttatal, a demonstration of verse forms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The Poetic Edda comes from the manuscript Codex Regius. Its first part has ten songs about gods, narrating the creation, destruction and rebirth of the mythological world, and its second has nineteen about heroes and heroines such as Sigurd, Brynhildr and Gunnar. Nothing is known of the manuscript's whereabouts until 1643, when it came into the possession of Brynjólfur Sveinsson, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Skálholt. Scholars had speculated that an Elder Edda once existed containing the pagan poems Snorri quotes, and the find seemed to confirm it. Brynjólfur attributed it to the 12th-century priest Sæmundr the Learned, a view modern scholars reject. He sent it to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-iv-of-denmark">Christian IV of Denmark</a>, and in 1971 it was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The richest source is the <b>Poetic Edda</b>, a collection of anonymous poems about the gods and heroes preserved mainly in a single Icelandic manuscript, the Codex Regius, written around 1270, which Denmark returned to Iceland in 1971.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The poems of the <b>Poetic Edda</b> told the myths of the gods and the legends of heroes such as Sigurd the dragon-slayer.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-norse">Old Norse · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Most of what is known of the myths was written down in Christian Iceland in the 13th century, in the poems of the <b>Poetic Edda</b> and in Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The Commonwealth produced the sagas, the <b>Eddas</b> and the histories of Snorri Sturluson.</blockquote><a href="/state/icelandic-commonwealth">Icelandic Commonwealth</a></li></ul></section>
